// The two ways a program says "this must not happen": one that ends the run, one
// that ends the compile.
//
// The file is `fail`, not `panic`, for a reason worth stating: the root
// re-exports each topic file as a namespace, and a namespace called `panic`
// sitting beside the prelude's `panic` *function* would be one name meaning two
// things. `str.nest` is left unexported for the same reason.

{ Location } :: import "loc.nest"
{ put, put_usize } :: import "write.nest"

// Abort the program with a message (§8). The `loc` default is what makes the
// report name the line that called `panic`, rather than this declaration: a
// default argument is filled in **at the call site**, so each call supplies its
// own position (§5.2). Without it every panic in the program would point here.
//
// The return type is `never`, which is the whole of why a `panic` may sit in any
// expression position: the signature *promises* divergence, so the type checker
// needs no special case for it (§3.1).
//
// This is an **ordinary function**, not an intrinsic. The compiler's own
// failures — an integer that overflowed under `overflow=trap`, an index past the
// end of a sequence — are lowered to a call to *this* function, found by its
// `#lang("panic")` tag, so a panic the compiler raises and a panic the program
// writes are the same code path and print the same way.
@public panic :: #lang("panic") func (msg: str, loc: Location := #caller_location) -> never {
  panic_handler(msg, loc)
}

// What a panic actually *does*, and the one piece of it a program may replace.
//
// `core` has no I/O — it does not know whether this target has a console, a
// serial port or nothing at all — so the default is the only thing that is
// correct everywhere: stop. A program that wants a message printed, a backtrace
// walked, or a reset vector jumped to declares its own:
//
//     #lang("panic_handler")
//     my_handler :: func (msg: str, loc: Location) -> never { ... }
//
// A `#lang` tag claimed outside `core` wins over `core`'s claim of the same tag
// (§9), which is what makes this a **default** rather than a fixed answer; two
// claims in the same package are still the duplicate error they always were.
// Set while a panic is being reported, so that a panic *inside* the report does
// not start another one.
//
// The cycle is real and not hypothetical: writing the digits below indexes an
// array, an index is bounds-checked, and a failed bounds check panics — which
// would arrive back here. One flag turns what would be unbounded recursion into
// a second line and a stop.
#static reporting: bool := false

@public panic_handler :: #lang("panic_handler") func (msg: str, loc: Location) -> never {
  if reporting {
    put("nest: panic while panicking\n")
    trap()
  }
  reporting = true
  put("nest: panic: ")
  put(msg)
  put("\n  at ")
  put(loc.file)
  put(":")
  put_usize(cast.<usize>(loc.line))
  put(":")
  put_usize(cast.<usize>(loc.column))
  put("\n")
  // The report is finished, so the flag that stopped a panic *inside* it has
  // nothing left to guard. It is cleared rather than left set because this is
  // not always the end: a guarded call returns below, and the next panic is a
  // first panic again.
  reporting = false
  nest_guard_fail()
  trap()
}

// Leave the guarded call, if a caller set one up — what lets a test binary
// report a failing test and run the next one (`nestc/src/lir/entry.rs`).
//
// It **returns** when nothing is guarding, which is every ordinary program: the
// `trap()` above is then reached exactly as it was before. A panic still does
// not unwind, so nothing between here and the guard runs on the way out.
//
// The name is the runtime's own symbol: an `extern("c")` declaration links by
// the name it is written with.
nest_guard_fail :: extern("c") func () -> void

// Stop the process, immediately and without unwinding — the one part of failing
// that no library can write, because it is a machine instruction (§6.4). Every
// target has one: `ud2`, `brk`, `unreachable`.
@public trap :: #intrinsic("trap") func () -> never

// A **compile-time** assertion (§6.10). The condition is evaluated during
// compilation and the program does not build if it is false; nothing is emitted
// either way, so this costs nothing at run time.
//
// The name says which one it is on purpose. `assert` below is the run-time one,
// and a reader should never have to know which file a name came from to know
// when it runs.
@public comptime_assert :: #intrinsic("comptime_assert") func (cond: bool) -> void

// A **run-time** assertion: if `cond` is false, panic with `msg`, reporting the
// line that called this rather than this line (§5.2).
//
// It is an ordinary function, not an intrinsic, and it goes through `panic` —
// so a program that replaced `#lang("panic_handler")` has replaced what a failed
// assertion does too. That is the whole reason not to make it an intrinsic: an
// assertion is a panic with a condition in front of it, and it should not be a
// second, less replaceable way to fail.
@public assert :: func (
  cond: bool,
  msg: str := "assertion failed",
  loc: Location := #caller_location,
) -> void {
  if cond == false { panic(msg, loc) }
}
